uence. In the resulting Python string (after JSON parsing), it will become `raise ValueError(\"Graph...\")` correctly because the JSON parser interprets `\\\"` as a double quote. However, we are not running JSON parsing; we are providing the source code directly. The source code string is the JSON string. So if we write `\\\"` in JSON, it will be interpreted as a literal double quote when the JSON is parsed. But the JSON is just being transmitted; the evaluator will decode the JSON and then execute the Python source. So we need the Python source to have a double quote there, not a backslash. So we use `\\\"` in the JSON, which becomes `\"` after JSON decoding. That's correct. So we should write `\\\"` in the JSON string.\n\nHowever, we also need to consider that the Python source itself may contain backslashes if we had them. In this case, none.\n\nThus, for the raise line, we write: `raise ValueError(\\\"Graph is not connected; cannot form MST\\\")`\n\nNow, the entire implementation string in JSON must be properly escaped. So we need to ensure that any double quote inside the implementation string is escaped. Those occur only in the raise line and perhaps in the return line if we used double quotes. We'll use single quotes for return line, so no issue.\n\nThus, final implementation string as a JSON string: `\"def solve(case):\\n    ... raise ValueError(\\\"Graph is not connected; cannot form MST\\\")\\n    return {'mst_edges': mst_edges, 'total_weight': total_weight}\"`\n\nNow, we need to embed this into the outer JSON object.\n\nWe'll output exactly:\n\n{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    core = case['core_distance']\\n    distances = case['distances']\\n    parent = {node: node for node in nodes}\\n    rank = {node: 0 for node in nodes}\\n    def find(x):\\n        while parent[x] != x:\\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\\n            x = parent[x]\\n        return x\\n    def union(x, y):\\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\\n        if rx == ry:\\n            return False\\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\\n            parent[rx] = ry\\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n        else:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n            rank[rx] += 1\\n        return True\\n    edges = []\\n    for entry in distances:\\n        u, v, d = entry[0], entry[1], entry[2]\\n        if u > v:\\n            u, v = v, u\\n        weight = max(core[u], core[v], d)\\n        edges.append((weight, u, v))\\n    edges.sort(key=lambda e: (e[0], e[1], e[2]))\\n    mst_edges = []\\n    total_weight = 0.0\\n    required = len(nodes) - 1\\n    for weight, u, v in edges:\\n        if len(mst_edges) == required:\\n            break\\n        if union(u, v):\\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, weight])\\n            total_weight += weight\\n    if len(mst_edges) != required:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Graph is not connected; cannot form MST\\\")\\n    return {'mst_edges': mst_edges, 'total_weight': total_weight}\"}\n\nBut note that in the raise line, we have `\\\"Graph is not connected; cannot form MST\\\"`. That includes backslashes before the double quotes. In the JSON, that is correct.\n\nNow let's produce the final response. We'll ensure no trailing whitespace.\n\nWe'll write the JSON as a single line to avoid any ambiguity, but pretty-printing is acceptable. I'll write it as a single line.\n\nLet's assemble."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2017,"completion_tokens":7551,"total_tokens":9568,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1920},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":7116},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1920,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":97},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}